{
  "id": "dl-arithmetic",
  "title": "Binary Arithmetic",
  "description": "Add binary numbers, represent negative numbers in two's complement, subtract and detect overflow.",
  "reference": "Hambley §7.2",
  "study": [
    {
      "title": "Binary addition",
      "body": "Add column by column from the right, exactly like decimal, but a column overflows already at 2: $1+1 = 10_2$ (sum 0, carry 1) and $1+1+1 = 11_2$ (sum 1, carry 1).\nExample: $0110 + 0111 = 1101$ (6 + 7 = 13).",
      "formula": "0+0=0,\\quad 0+1=1,\\quad 1+1=10_2,\\quad 1+1+1=11_2"
    },
    {
      "title": "One's and two's complement",
      "body": "The **one's complement** inverts every bit. The **two's complement** is the one's complement plus 1 (ignore any carry out of the MSB). Shortcut: copy the bits from the right up to and including the first 1, then invert all remaining bits.\nExample: $00101100$ (44) → one's complement $11010011$ → two's complement $11010100$, which represents −44.",
      "formula": "\\text{two's complement} = \\overline{\\text{bits}} + 1"
    },
    {
      "title": "Signed two's-complement numbers",
      "body": "In an $n$-bit signed word the MSB is the **sign bit** (0 = positive, 1 = negative) and has weight $-2^{n-1}$. A negative number is stored as the two's complement of its magnitude. There is one zero, and the range is lopsided: 8 bits cover −128 … +127.",
      "formula": "-2^{n-1} \\;\\le\\; N \\;\\le\\; 2^{n-1}-1"
    },
    {
      "title": "Subtraction",
      "body": "To compute $A - B$, add the two's complement of $B$ to $A$ and discard any carry out of the sign bit. The same adder hardware therefore does both addition and subtraction.\nExample (8 bits): $45 - 19$: $00101101 + 11101101 = (1)\\,00011010 = 26$.",
      "formula": "A - B = A + (\\overline{B} + 1)"
    },
    {
      "title": "Overflow",
      "body": "Overflow (called underflow when the true result is too negative) happens when the true result lies outside the range of the word. Rule: it can only occur when both operands have the **same sign**, and it shows up as a result with the **opposite sign**. Adding numbers of opposite sign never overflows.\nExample (4 bits): $0101 + 0100 = 1001$, i.e. 5 + 4 gives −7. A carry out of the MSB by itself is **not** an overflow indicator."
    }
  ],
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "dl-arithmetic-1",
      "title": "Adding and negating",
      "kind": "learn",
      "steps": [
        {
          "id": "i1",
          "type": "info",
          "title": "Adding bits",
          "body": "Binary addition works column by column with carries, just like decimal: $0+1 = 1$, $1+1 = 10_2$ (write 0, carry 1), $1+1+1 = 11_2$ (write 1, carry 1).",
          "formula": "\\begin{array}{r} 0110 \\\\ +\\,0011 \\\\ \\hline 1001 \\end{array}"
        },
        {
          "id": "q1",
          "type": "multipleChoice",
          "prompt": "Add ${{a3}}{{a2}}{{a1}}{{a0}}_2 + {{b3}}{{b2}}{{b1}}{{b0}}_2$.",
